Long summer series, through the life and ministry and teachings of the Apostle Paul.

17
00:01:04,080 --> 00:01:09,300
And our desire is that as we continue this series, that we would better understand who

18
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:12,520
Paul is because he is the writer of a majority of the New Testament.

19
00:01:12,520 --> 00:01:18,620
So that as we read through his letters, we can better understand the context, the story

20
00:01:18,620 --> 00:01:23,560
behind the author that God chose by the power of his spirit to breathe through so much of

21
00:01:23,560 --> 00:01:25,060
our New Testament Scriptures.

22
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:27,180
We looked at the biography of his life.

23
00:01:27,180 --> 00:01:29,780
He started a persecutor of the church.

24
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:37,120
He started hating the church and had a radical encounter with Jesus, which led him into a deep,

25
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:41,880
deep relationship with the Lord that fueled a passion for the church.

26
00:01:41,880 --> 00:01:46,240
He went on missionary journeys to plant and to strengthen churches.

27
00:01:47,640 --> 00:01:54,140
And ultimately, he went on a missionary journey at the end of his life where he voluntarily went

28
00:01:54,140 --> 00:01:59,360
to prison so that he could get to Rome to share the gospel with those who might not

29
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:00,020
hear it otherwise.

30
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:03,520
He sacrificed his entire life for the gospel of Jesus.

31
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:05,680
We looked at his biography.

32
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:09,900
And last week, we started, we're going to go through about the next four weeks, pastoral

33
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:13,780
kind of teachings and contributions that he made to the church.

34
00:02:13,780 --> 00:02:20,040
And we started last week by saying what Paul, how Paul talked about living missionally, living

35
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:25,080
towards people who are outside the family of God, people who aren't yet brought into the

36
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:28,240
family of God, who haven't yet submitted their lives to Jesus.

37
00:02:28,240 --> 00:02:35,540
And we said that Paul's, his whole method for living missionally was to lay down his priorities,

38
00:02:35,540 --> 00:02:41,480
his privileges, and his propensities to win an argument in order to win a person.

39
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:43,780
Like he laid it all down.

40
00:02:43,780 --> 00:02:48,560
Everything that he had, everything that he was able to claim, he said, none of it is worth

41
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:51,480
the person who is outside the family of God.

42
00:02:51,480 --> 00:02:52,560
We will lay it all down.

43
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:58,420
He calls, he invites all of us to do that, to live in a way that invites people into a right

44
00:02:58,420 --> 00:02:59,800
relationship with God.

45
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:07,060
And this week, we're going to look at a natural consequence of what happens when we are successful

46
00:03:07,060 --> 00:03:07,740
at this.

47
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:12,740
Like when we are successful at reaching people who are outside the family of God and bringing

48
00:03:12,740 --> 00:03:17,160
them inside the family of God, people from different ethnicities, different backgrounds,

49
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:20,420
different theologies, different ideologies.

50
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:25,900
Like when we reach a diverse group of people, guess what the church typically has a problem

51
00:03:25,900 --> 00:03:26,480
with next?

52
00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:29,160
Unity.

53
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:30,700
Right?

54
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:36,220
We struggle with unity as we reach a diverse background of people because we're good with

55
00:03:36,220 --> 00:03:39,700
people that look like us, talk like us, act like us, all of that.

56
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:39,880
Right?

57
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:45,620
But if we do what Paul calls us to do, if we lay down our priorities and privileges and propensities

58
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:51,380
to win arguments, we will win people who disagree with us on major consequential issues.

59
00:03:52,220 --> 00:03:57,960
And yet Paul, throughout his writings, is constantly hammering home the importance of the church

60
00:03:57,960 --> 00:03:58,740
being unified.

61
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:02,920
It's one of the last things that Jesus prayed before he went to the cross, that the world

62
00:04:02,920 --> 00:04:07,660
will know that we are his disciples by our love for one another.

63
00:04:07,660 --> 00:04:15,380
And yet we struggle with this as people come into the family of God that look different than

64
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:15,600
us.

65
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:17,760
And so, we talked about living missionally.

66
00:04:17,760 --> 00:04:22,960
This week, we're going to talk about how do we have unity as we reach people who are far

67
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:23,280
from God.

68
00:04:23,280 --> 00:04:23,880
Sound like a plan?

69
00:04:23,880 --> 00:04:25,440
All right, let's pray.

70
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:30,900
Holy Spirit, we invite you into this place.

71
00:04:30,900 --> 00:04:38,740
We know that you have been this week preparing all of us to hear and to receive, not just the

72
00:04:38,740 --> 00:04:43,240
message from stage, but through the worship and through interactions and prayers in the

73
00:04:43,240 --> 00:04:44,540
lobby and in this place.

74
00:04:44,620 --> 00:04:50,740
God, we know that you have a desire to conform us into your image, to conform us to the image

75
00:04:50,740 --> 00:04:51,140
of Jesus.

76
00:04:51,140 --> 00:04:54,880
We want this hour to do that in some way, shape, or form.

77
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:57,140
We believe that you will do that.

78
00:04:57,140 --> 00:04:58,240
We're inviting you to do that.

79
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:03,540
May we not have hard hearts, not have dull ears.

80
00:05:03,540 --> 00:05:06,880
May we open ourselves up before you.

81
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:14,340
May you transform us from the inside out, not some kind of religious checkbox where we

82
00:05:14,340 --> 00:05:20,140
just do the things that you say to do begrudgingly, but may you give us the deep, deep desire to

83
00:05:20,140 --> 00:05:25,140
find unity with our brothers and sisters that we disagree with on difficult issues.

84
00:05:27,540 --> 00:05:29,900
God, we believe that your church is most effective.

85
00:05:29,900 --> 00:05:33,160
I believe that your church is most effective when we are most unified.

86
00:05:33,160 --> 00:05:39,940
And I believe that we have the temptation, the struggle to not pursue that.

87
00:05:40,700 --> 00:05:42,700
So may this morning be a great reminder.

88
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:47,320
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

89
00:05:47,320 --> 00:05:47,960
Amen.

90
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:52,820
If you have Bibles, I'd love for you to turn to Romans chapter 14.

91
00:05:52,820 --> 00:05:56,120
If you have a phone app or whatever, Romans chapter 14.

92
00:05:56,120 --> 00:05:59,680
I'm going to be reading from the ESV in a little bit so that you can follow along with

93
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:07,100
me, one of the biggest privileges I get to participate in as a pastor is something that is called premarital

94
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:07,560
counseling.

95
00:06:07,560 --> 00:06:09,900
Any of you ever experienced premarital counseling?

96
00:06:09,900 --> 00:06:11,580
Some of you?

97
00:06:11,580 --> 00:06:11,900
All right.

98
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:16,020
I'm not making eye contact with the couple in the front row who is currently in premarital

99
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:16,760
counseling with me.

100
00:06:16,760 --> 00:06:17,940
Don't worry.

101
00:06:17,940 --> 00:06:21,960
What's shared in premarital counseling stays in premarital counseling.

102
00:06:21,960 --> 00:06:24,120
This will not be any of your stories.

103
00:06:24,120 --> 00:06:25,280
Probably.

104
00:06:25,280 --> 00:06:26,080
No, I'm just kidding.

105
00:06:27,140 --> 00:06:31,180
But here's the thing about premarital counseling, where I often start with in premarital counseling

106
00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:35,840
is I often ask people, like, what were the cultural norms in your family?

107
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:39,280
How did things operate in your family of origin?

108
00:06:39,280 --> 00:06:45,060
And then identify which of those things do you like, dislike, agree with, want to stay the

109
00:06:45,060 --> 00:06:46,780
same, want to change?

110
00:06:46,780 --> 00:06:52,480
And it's important that we have these dialogues because we all have norms in our family.

111
00:06:53,320 --> 00:06:57,300
Whether you know it or not, there is a normal experience for your family.

112
00:06:57,300 --> 00:07:03,200
And oftentimes when two people come together from different families, they are on a crash

113
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:08,500
course to realize that what they thought was normal to them wasn't necessarily normal to

114
00:07:08,500 --> 00:07:09,160
the other person.

115
00:07:09,160 --> 00:07:09,800
Does that make sense?

116
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:14,660
One of my favorite, like, little examples of this is maybe you grew up in a home where

117
00:07:14,660 --> 00:07:17,580
the norm was if you did the cooking, you don't do the dishes.

118
00:07:17,580 --> 00:07:21,340
That's, like, somewhat normal for some families, right?

119
00:07:21,340 --> 00:07:28,200
Now, if I don't find ways to forge those conversations with a young couple before they get married,

120
00:07:28,200 --> 00:07:36,360
if that was not the norm for the young man in the house, and the new lovely wife cooks dinner,

121
00:07:36,360 --> 00:07:41,960
wonderful dinner, and the man enjoys it and sits down on the couch and props his feet up,

122
00:07:42,060 --> 00:07:44,380
and for the wife, the norm was, I'm not doing the dishes.

123
00:07:44,380 --> 00:07:48,700
That man is on a collision course for conflict, right?

124
00:07:48,700 --> 00:07:55,860
And this is, like, this is a small illustration of what happens on such a bigger scale

125
00:07:55,860 --> 00:08:02,480
when we reach diverse people coming into the family of God, because the church is the family of God.

126
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:08,200
And as people come in with diverse backgrounds, diverse experiences, ideological thoughts,

127
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:13,200
different worldviews, all of that stuff, we're all submitted to the Lord Jesus, but we don't become

128
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:16,620
the same overnight, right?

129
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:22,700
And so what happens in the church is we come into this place, we reach people who are different than us,

130
00:08:22,700 --> 00:08:25,720
and we immediately find tension.

131
00:08:25,720 --> 00:08:29,660
We find conflict because somebody sitting next to us thinks something differently than we do,

132
00:08:29,660 --> 00:08:33,800
or they see something from a different perspective than we do, and we say, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

133
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:36,520
That's not how this thing works.

134
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:37,940
This is how this thing works.

135
00:08:37,940 --> 00:08:43,780
And so we end up fighting over things that we shouldn't be fighting over.

136
00:08:43,780 --> 00:08:47,220
We end up getting confused over things that shouldn't be confusing,

137
00:08:47,220 --> 00:08:52,780
because we're fixated on things that are less important than the fact that we've all submitted

138
00:08:52,780 --> 00:08:53,840
to Jesus as Lord.

139
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:58,680
And so we want to pursue unity.

140
00:08:58,680 --> 00:09:02,640
We want to have intentional conversations about our perspectives and our backgrounds.

141
00:09:02,640 --> 00:09:07,080
I love Austin Fisher as a pastor I really admire, and he says this,

142
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:12,760
the church at its best is the most unifying social organization in world history.

143
00:09:14,040 --> 00:09:16,020
Notice he says, at its best.

144
00:09:16,020 --> 00:09:18,240
This is what Christ intended.

145
00:09:18,240 --> 00:09:20,160
When he prayed, you will be one.

146
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:24,380
The world will know that you are my disciples when you are one.

147
00:09:24,380 --> 00:09:28,740
The church at its best is the most unifying social organization in world history,

148
00:09:28,740 --> 00:09:30,720
and yet as we look throughout church history,

149
00:09:30,720 --> 00:09:37,140
we see divisions and denominations and splits over and over and over again.

150
00:09:37,220 --> 00:09:42,720
And I think it's possible that we're missing out on the fullness of what God has for his church.

151
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:48,120
And so in our passage today, we're going to look at a central part of Paul's teaching

152
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:50,220
to a diverse and divided church.

153
00:09:50,220 --> 00:09:54,540
The church at Rome was full of Jews and Gentiles from different backgrounds

154
00:09:54,540 --> 00:09:59,360
and different ideologies and different socioeconomic statuses,

155
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:02,220
and they were having trouble finding unity.

156
00:10:03,440 --> 00:10:08,040
They were getting in conflicts over things that to us will look silly,

157
00:10:08,040 --> 00:10:10,560
but to them very much were not silly.

158
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:15,840
So before we jump specifically into the passage of Romans 14,

159
00:10:15,840 --> 00:10:20,120
I want to do a quick 30,000-foot overview of the entire book of Romans

160
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:22,260
so we don't miss Paul's overarching point.

161
00:10:23,100 --> 00:10:27,020
I like to imagine, especially because we're going to be taking communion at the end of service,

162
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:32,080
I like to imagine that the book of Romans was written to a group of diverse,

163
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:38,020
divided Christians around a dinner table with Jesus at the head of the table.

164
00:10:38,020 --> 00:10:42,140
This is God's image for the church.

165
00:10:42,140 --> 00:10:46,320
We are all supposed to be sitting around one table with Jesus at the head.

166
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:51,740
In Romans 1-11, the first half of Romans, Paul says,

167
00:10:51,740 --> 00:10:54,560
look to Jesus at the head of the table.

168
00:10:54,560 --> 00:11:01,460
Remember who he is, what he is like, and why he invited you to the table in the first place,

169
00:11:01,460 --> 00:11:02,640
how you got there.

170
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:06,840
Romans 1-11 is all about looking to Jesus at the head of the table.

171
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:08,900
He has brought you into the family of God,

172
00:11:08,900 --> 00:11:13,300
not by works, but by faith in the Lord Jesus.

173
00:11:13,300 --> 00:11:16,620
No matter what background you come from, slave, free, Jew, Gentile,

174
00:11:16,620 --> 00:11:21,520
he has brought, he has invited all of us to sit at the same table by faith in the Lord Jesus.

175
00:11:21,520 --> 00:11:23,760
Look to Jesus at the head of the table.

176
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:25,900
That's the first section of Romans.

177
00:11:25,900 --> 00:11:28,100
And then he says, okay, now with that in mind,

178
00:11:28,100 --> 00:11:31,200
with that vision of you looking to Jesus at the head of the table,

179
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:33,380
now look around at one another.

180
00:11:33,380 --> 00:11:38,100
With the same perspective that Jesus looks at you,

181
00:11:38,100 --> 00:11:42,380
look to one another with the same eyes that Jesus looks to you.

182
00:11:42,460 --> 00:11:46,260
And Paul believes that a right understanding of who God is

183
00:11:46,260 --> 00:11:49,520
will lead us to a right relationship with one another.

184
00:11:49,520 --> 00:11:54,220
Pretty simple concept, right?

185
00:11:54,220 --> 00:11:57,420
The book of Romans is not that hard to understand.

186
00:11:57,420 --> 00:11:59,060
However, much of the American church,

187
00:11:59,060 --> 00:12:02,160
we love to study the theology found in Romans.

188
00:12:02,160 --> 00:12:07,940
But we often lose sight of why Paul is going so depth in the theology in the first place.

189
00:12:07,940 --> 00:12:10,000
Unity.

190
00:12:11,520 --> 00:12:14,560
And instead of walking away with Paul's intended vision,

191
00:12:14,560 --> 00:12:17,220
we often walk away more divided than ever

192
00:12:17,220 --> 00:12:20,720
because we just get in fights over what chapters 1 through 11 say

193
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:24,520
without ever getting to the application of chapters 12 through 16.

194
00:12:24,520 --> 00:12:28,480
There's actually a scholar that I love.

195
00:12:28,480 --> 00:12:30,640
If you ever get the chance to read a book by Scott McKnight,

196
00:12:30,640 --> 00:12:31,800
I'd encourage you to do so.

197
00:12:31,800 --> 00:12:33,900
Scott McKnight just recently wrote a book called

198
00:12:33,900 --> 00:12:36,080
Reading Romans Backward.

199
00:12:36,560 --> 00:12:41,400
And his whole point is that the theology that Paul lays out in 1 through 11,

200
00:12:41,400 --> 00:12:43,680
the whole point of the theology in 1 through 11

201
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:47,780
is to give a foundation for the application in chapters 12 through 16.

202
00:12:47,780 --> 00:12:50,800
So he says, if you start by reading 12 through 16,

203
00:12:50,800 --> 00:12:55,220
understanding what Paul's actually trying to communicate in his theology,

204
00:12:55,220 --> 00:12:58,940
then we will be less tempted to miss the mark.

205
00:12:58,980 --> 00:13:03,140
But instead, we get so fixated on the beauty, the depth, the theology in 1 through 11,

206
00:13:03,140 --> 00:13:06,360
and we just like, oh yeah, we'll get to 12 through 16 later.

207
00:13:06,360 --> 00:13:10,020
But the whole point of Paul writing the book of Romans

208
00:13:10,020 --> 00:13:12,140
was the application in 12 through 16.

209
00:13:12,140 --> 00:13:15,480
The whole reason he slaved on the letter to Romans

210
00:13:15,480 --> 00:13:19,480
was so that this divided group of believers would be united.

211
00:13:19,480 --> 00:13:25,760
And somehow, we read this book and walk away more divided than ever.

212
00:13:25,760 --> 00:13:29,440
Scott summarizes his point here.

213
00:13:29,440 --> 00:13:29,680
He says,

214
00:13:29,680 --> 00:13:33,060
Paul's mission was to establish churches in the Roman Empire

215
00:13:33,060 --> 00:13:38,260
made up of Jews and Gentiles, slaves and free, male and female.

216
00:13:38,400 --> 00:13:42,840
His fear was denominations, one Jewish, one Gentile.

217
00:13:42,840 --> 00:13:45,440
And his message was peace.

218
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:48,080
This is the point of the book of Romans,

219
00:13:48,080 --> 00:13:51,140
that we would be united in the midst of our consequential differences.

220
00:13:51,140 --> 00:13:53,600
It's not that the things that we divide over don't matter.

221
00:13:53,600 --> 00:13:55,760
It's that they don't matter as much as unity.

222
00:13:55,760 --> 00:14:03,120
I know brothers and sisters that I love and I'm thankful for

223
00:14:03,120 --> 00:14:06,760
who literally, they have a master's degree on Romans 1 through 11.

224
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:12,100
But if you look at the way that they treat one another around the table,

225
00:14:12,100 --> 00:14:15,300
you would never know that they've even read 12 through 16.

226
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:21,420
And I know brothers and sisters who are the most kind, compassionate, and loving people

227
00:14:21,420 --> 00:14:25,160
who are obsessed with the commands in chapters 12 through 16.

228
00:14:25,160 --> 00:14:30,860
They're obsessed with the idea about loving people well.

229
00:14:30,860 --> 00:14:35,340
But they don't take enough time to look at Jesus at the head of the table

230
00:14:35,340 --> 00:14:39,100
and they end up loving people in a way that doesn't actually mirror the love of Jesus.

231
00:14:39,100 --> 00:14:46,980
It's a surface level, superficial type of love for one another that isn't rooted in who Jesus is.

232
00:14:46,980 --> 00:14:51,720
And others, this is the most heartbreaking version,

233
00:14:51,720 --> 00:14:54,920
others are content to leave the table where Jesus is at the head

234
00:14:54,920 --> 00:14:59,060
and start their own little table full of people that look exactly like them,

235
00:14:59,060 --> 00:15:01,620
talk exactly like them, act exactly like them,

236
00:15:01,660 --> 00:15:03,100
and believe exactly like them.

